<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sadashivrao Bhau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1730">1730</a>–<a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sadashivrao Bhau was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> general and cousin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-baji-rao">Balaji Baji Rao</a>. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> at Udgir in 1760, gaining large territories, and then commanded the Maratha army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau.jpg" alt="Sadashivrao Bhau" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deccani School · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_portrait_in_an_oval_of_the_Bhao,_Sadhashir_Rao,.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sadashivrao Bhau was born on 4 August 1730 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> into a Marathi Chitpavan Brahmin family, the son of Chimaji Appa, younger brother of Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a>, and Rakhmabai of the Pethe family. His mother died when he was about a month old and his father when he was ten, and he was raised by his grandmother Radhabai and his aunt Kashibai. He served as a finance minister during the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> ruler Rajaram II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first campaign was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in 1746. He won his first battle at Ajra, south of Kolhapur, and took thirty-six parganas. In a later Carnatic campaign he conquered territory from the Nawab of Savanur and put down the revolt of Yamaji Shivdev.</p><p>Having seen the effect of artillery and light guns in British hands, he built similar units for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> army. In 1760 he engaged Ibrahim Khan Gardi, a former officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, who brought 2,500 trained soldiers and fifteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. Within two years the infantry and artillery division had grown to 10,000 men and 56 guns.</p><p>Appointed to lead the army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Abdali</a>, he set out from Patdur on 14 March 1760 with about 55,000 men and roughly 200,000 non-combatants, including families and pilgrims. Unlike Malharrao Holkar and Raghunathrao he did not know the politics of the north. He disregarded the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a>, the Jat ruler of Bharatpur, to leave the civilians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. The army took Delhi on 2 August 1760 and then stormed the fort of Kunjpura, killing its entire garrison.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abdali crossed the swollen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> at Baghpat between 23 and 25 October 1760, surprised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and cut their supply lines. A two-month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> near Panipat brought famine to the camp, where many civilians and animals died. On 14 January 1761 the Marathas tried to break out. After his nephew Vishwasrao, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s heir, was killed around 2 p.m., Sadashivrao reportedly left his elephant and charged on horseback; the empty howdah led some troops to think him dead. He died fighting, and the remaining civilians were massacred. Some records say his headless body was recovered three days later, others that it was never found.</p><p>His widow Parvatibai, who had accompanied the campaign, refused the conventional life of widowhood. Around 1770 a man appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> claiming to be Sadashivrao; he was exposed and became known as Totayaa, the impostor. The Sadashiv Peth quarter of Pune is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sadashivrao Bhau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1759 his army had reached Lahore, and on 14 January 1761 it decisively defeated the Marathas under <b>Sadashivrao Bhau</b> at the Third Battle of Panipat, fought along a twelve-kilometre front between largely Muslim and largely Hindu armies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Panipat, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.52,73.86&amp;year=1750" aria-label="Pune on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6551 1477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sadashivrao Bhau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7001 1747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7001 1747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.52,73.86&amp;year=1750">What was here in 1750? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
